   792                              <1> .ubpbNotSCP:
   793                              <1> ;Now blind check version numbers for 16-bit DOS OEM strings.
   794                              <1> ;Search for DOS 2.0, 3.1 and 3.2. These versions have ID's such that 
   795                              <1> ; the version number starts at position 5.
   796                              <1> ;Usually, these BPBs have MSDOS or IBM<SPC><SPC> or OEM<SPC><SPC>
   797                              <1> ; as the OEM string. OEM stands for any three characters used by an
   798                              <1> ; OEM to identify disks they formatted, follows by "x.y" for a major
   799                              <1> ; and minor version number.
   800                              <1> ;Since we cannot enumerate all the OEM strings and can't guarantee
   801                              <1> ; that they have the bug, we will simply check the version number @
   802                              <1> ; position 5 in the string which seems to have been an unofficial 
   803                              <1> ; standard for placing the version number in the OEM string at the 
   804                              <1> ; time. 
   805                              <1> ;The dot in the check helps "guarantee" the number is a version number 
   806                              <1> ; we are possibly interested in.
   807 000008CC 66817B08332E        <1>     cmp word [rbx + oemHeader.oemString + 5], "3."  ;DOS 3?
